    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d83">

                <pb facs="0150" n="122" xml:id="pg122"/>

                <head>83. National Security Action Memorandum No. 221<anchor n="0" xml:id="d83-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1963-02-20">February 20,
                        1963</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list type="to">
                    <head>MEMORANDUM TO</head>
                    <item>The Secretary of State</item>
                    <item>The Secretary of Defense</item>
                    <item>The Director of Central Intelligence</item>
                    <item>The Administrator, Agency for International Development</item>
                    <item>The Director, United States Information Agency</item>
                    <item>The Director, Peace Corps</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>U.S. Policy Toward Algeria</item>
                </list>
                <p>I generally approve the Plan of Action for Algeria in the Secretary of State’s
                    memorandum to me of 2 February 1963, responding to NSAM 211.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d83-f2"/></p>
                <p>All concerned should bear in mind the need for a program commensurate with our
                    policy interests in this key North African country. While we want France to
                    shoulder the lion’s share of the burden, we also want to increase our own
                    influence. For example, I should like to be advised if it should appear highly
                    desirable to exceed somewhat the $15 million FY 1964 AID level currently
                    projected.</p>
                <p>Since Prime Minister Ben Bella has requested assistance from the Peace Corps and
                    the Algerians need just the sort of help the Peace Corps is peculiarly suited to
                    give, I hope it will be able to respond if other commitments permit.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">John F. Kennedy</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
